WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.600
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:10.599
This episode is about a classic, the Dunning-Kruger
effect. I wonder how we could go on for almost

00:00:10.599 --> 00:00:14.110
60 Two Minute Papers episodes without the
Dunning-Kruger effect?

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:19.540
Here is the experiment: participants were
tested in different subjects, their test scores

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:24.680
were computed, and at the same time, without
the scores, they were asked to assess their

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.330
perceived performance.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:32.279
The test subjects were humor, grammar, and
logic. Things, of course, everyone excels

00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:34.070
at ... or do they?

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:40.340
And here is the historic plot with the results.
Such a simple plot, yet it tells us so much

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:45.710
about people. From left to right, people were
ordered by their score as you see with the

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:51.470
dotted line. And the other line with the squares
shows their perceived score, what they thought

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:56.750
their scores would be. People from the bottom
10 percent, the absolute worst performers

00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:00.280
are convinced that they were well above the
average.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:05.110
Competent people, on the other hand, seemed
to underestimate their skills. Because the

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:09.780
test was easy for them, they assumed that
it was easy for everyone else.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:15.220
The extreme to the left is often referred
to as the Dunning-Kruger effect, and the extreme

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:21.830
to the right, maybe if you imagine the lines
extending way-way further, is a common example

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:23.600
of impostor syndrome.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:28.490
By the way, everyone thinks they are above
average, which is neat mathematical anomaly.

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:33.650
We would expect that people who perform poorly
should know that they perform poorly, and

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:38.460
people who're doing great should know they're
doing great. One of the conclusions is that

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:41.650
this is not the case, not the case at all.

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:47.580
The fact that incompetent people are completely
ignorant about their own inadequacy, at first,

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:52.479
sounds like such a surprising conclusion.
But if we think about it, we find there's

00:01:52.479 --> 00:01:57.829
nothing surprising about this. The more skilled
we are, the more adept we are at estimating

00:01:57.829 --> 00:02:02.869
our skill level. By gaining more competence,
incompetent people also obtained the skill

00:02:02.869 --> 00:02:05.210
to recognize their own shortcomings.

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:12.030
A fish, in the world of Poker, means an inadequate
player who is to be extorted by the more experienced.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:17.830
Someone asked how to recognize who the fish
is at a poker table. The answer is a classic:

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:21.390
if you don't know who the fish is at the table,
it is you.

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:26.500
The knowledge of the Dunning-Kruger effect
is such a tempting tool to condemn other people

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:32.480
for their inadequacy. But please, try to resist
the temptation, remember, it doesn't help,

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.510
that's the point of the paper! It is a much
more effective tool for our own development

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:43.709
if we attempt to use it on ourselves. Does
it hurt a bit more? Oh yes, it does! The results

00:02:43.709 --> 00:02:49.900
of this paper solidify the argument that we
need to be very vigilant about our own shortcomings.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:55.120
This knowledge endows you with a shield against
ignorance. Use it wisely.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.040
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

